<topic xml:id="rmt-configuring-with-ansible"
 role="task" xml:lang="en"
 xmlns="http://docbook.org/ns/docbook" version="5.2"
 xmlns:its="http://www.w3.org/2005/11/its"
 xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude"
 xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink"
 xmlns:trans="http://docbook.org/ns/transclusion">
  <info>
    <title>Configuring &rmt; with &ansible;</title>
    <meta name="maintainer" content="amrita.sakthivel@suse.com" its:translate="no"/>
    <abstract>
         <para>
          Configure &rmt; with &ansible; as described in the following procedure.
          The <package>ansible-rmt-server</package> package provides pre-built
          playbooks and roles for automated &rmt; configuration.
        </para>
        </abstract>
  </info>

 <procedure>
  <step>
<para>
Install the <package>ansible-rmt-server</package> package:
</para>
<stepalternatives>
<step>
<para>
On a mutable system:
</para>
<screen>&prompt.root;<command>zypper in ansible-rmt-server</command></screen>
</step>
<step os="sles">
<para>
On an immutable system, also install the packages that the
playbook requires, then reboot the system to switch to the new
snapshot. If you skip these packages, the playbook installs
them into a new snapshot that is not active until the next
reboot, and the services that the playbook starts are missing.
</para>
<screen>&prompt.root;<command>transactional-update pkg install ansible-rmt-server mariadb nginx python3-PyMySQL</command>
&prompt.root;<command>reboot</command></screen>
</step>
</stepalternatives>
</step>
    <step>
      <para>
        Navigate to the &ansible; configuration directory:
      </para>
<screen>&prompt.root;<command>cd /usr/share/ansible/rmt</command></screen>
    </step>

    <step>
      <para>
       Create the configuration file based on the provided example:
      </para>
<stepalternatives>
<step>
<para>
On a mutable system, create <filename>group_vars/all.yml</filename>:
</para>
<screen>&prompt.root;<command>cp group_vars/all.yml.example group_vars/all.yml</command></screen>
</step>
<step os="sles">
<para>
On an immutable system, <filename>/usr</filename> is read-only.
Create the configuration file in a writable location, for example
<filename>/root/rmt-vars.yml</filename>, and make it readable only
by &rootuser;, because it contains credentials:
</para>
<screen>&prompt.root;<command>install -m 600 group_vars/all.yml.example /root/rmt-vars.yml</command></screen>
</step>
</stepalternatives>
</step>

    <step>
      <para>
Edit the configuration file and configure the following variables:
      </para>
      <variablelist>
        <varlistentry>
          <term><literal>rmt_db_username</literal></term>
          <listitem>
            <para>
              Database user name for &rmt; (default: <literal>rmt</literal>)
            </para>
          </listitem>
        </varlistentry>
        <varlistentry>
          <term><literal>rmt_db_password</literal></term>
          <listitem>
            <para>
              Secure password for the &rmt; database user
            </para>
          </listitem>
        </varlistentry>
        <varlistentry>
          <term><literal>RMT_ORGANIZATION_USERNAME</literal></term>
          <listitem>
            <para>
              Your &scc; organization credentials. To retrieve your
              credentials, log in to
              <link xlink:href="https://scc.suse.com/">Mirroring credentials</link>.
            </para>
          </listitem>
        </varlistentry>
        <varlistentry>
          <term><literal>RMT_ORGANIZATION_PASSWORD</literal></term>
          <listitem>
            <para>
              Your &scc; organization password
            </para>
          </listitem>
        </varlistentry>
      </variablelist>

      <para>
        Example configuration:
      </para>
<screen>---
rmt_db_username: rmt
rmt_db_password: "your_secure_password"

rmt:
  RMT_ORGANIZATION_USERNAME: "your_scc_username"
  RMT_ORGANIZATION_PASSWORD: "your_scc_password"</screen>
    </step>

    <step>
      <para>
        Run the &ansible; playbook to configure &rmt;:
      </para>
<para os="sles">
On an immutable system, pass the configuration file that you created
in <filename>/root</filename> as extra variables:</para>
<screen os="sles">&prompt.root;<command>ansible-playbook site.yml -e @/root/rmt-vars.yml</command></screen>
      <para>
        The playbook performs the following tasks:
      </para>
      <itemizedlist>
        <listitem>
          <para>Install required dependencies (MariaDB, nginx, Python modules)</para>
        </listitem>
        <listitem>
          <para>Configure and start the MariaDB database service</para>
        </listitem>
        <listitem>
          <para>Create the &rmt; database and database user</para>
        </listitem>
        <listitem>
          <para>Generate SSL certificates for HTTPS</para>
        </listitem>
        <listitem>
          <para>Configure nginx virtual hosts</para>
        </listitem>
        <listitem>
          <para>Create <filename>/etc/rmt.conf</filename> configuration file</para>
        </listitem>
        <listitem>
          <para>Run database migrations</para>
        </listitem>
        <listitem>
          <para>Start and enable the &rmt; server service</para>
        </listitem>
      </itemizedlist>
    </step>

    <step>
      <para>
        On systems with &selnx; in enforcing mode, allow nginx to connect to
        the &rmt; back-end service:
      </para>
<screen>&prompt.root;<command>setsebool -P httpd_can_network_connect 1</command></screen>
    </step>

    <step>
      <para>
        Verify the installation by checking the API health status:
      </para>
<screen>&prompt.root;<command>curl -s http://localhost/api/health/status</command>
{"state":"online"}</screen>
    </step>
  </procedure>

  <tip>
    <title>SSL certificate locations</title>
    <para>
      The &ansible; playbook generates SSL certificates at the following
      locations:
    </para>
<variablelist>
<varlistentry>
<term>CA certificate</term>
<listitem><para> <filename>/etc/rmt/ssl/rmt-ca.crt</filename></para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
  <term>CA private key</term>
  <listitem><para> <filename>/etc/rmt/ssl/rmt-ca.key</filename></para></listitem>
  </varlistentry>
  <varlistentry>
    <term>Server certificate</term>
    <listitem><para> <filename>/etc/rmt/ssl/rmt-server.crt</filename></para></listitem>
    </varlistentry>
    <varlistentry>
      <term>Server private key</term>
      <listitem><para> <filename>/etc/rmt/ssl/rmt-server.key</filename></para></listitem>
      </varlistentry>
</variablelist>
</tip>
</topic>
